  "query_preview": "/MONDAY THROUGH FRIDAY/...\nIssued at 255 PM MDT Fri Aug 28 2026\n\nThe long term period is shaping up to be fairly consistent with \nunseasonably hot temperatures and evening storm chances due to \nour region being in a persistent southwest upper-level flow. \nMonday and Tuesday, high temperatures are forecast in the 80s \nto upper 90s. Wednesday through Friday, highs are currently \nforecast in the high 90s with LREF ensemble members showing at \nleast a 20% chance of high exceeding 100 degrees F for our \nKansas and Nebraska counties. \n\nOur persistent southwest upper-level flow will send several embedded \nshortwaves, bringing precipitation chances each evening Monday \nthrough Friday. Monday is looking to be out best chance for \nprecipitation with Probability of Precipitation (PoPs) from 30-60%, \nincreasing from east to west. Models are showing a marginally \nunstable environment with less than 1000 J/Kg of CAPE, around 30\nknots of shear and mid-low level lapse rates less than 7 C/km. \nModel soundings are showing precipitable water (PWAT) values \ngreater than 1 inch and overall skinny CAPE profiles, so heavy \nrainfall and localized flooding are possible with severe or sub-\nsevere storms. Small hail and strong winds could be a concern \nwith stronger storms if they are able to form. Tuesday onward, \nevening PoPs are around 20% for the area with low confidence in \nsevere weather occurring. \n\nDespite unseasonably hot temperatures, fire weather concerns are \nminimal through the long term period. Relative humidity (RH) values \nare forecast above 20% for the CWA. Winds will remain overall light \nwith gusts maxing out around 20 mph.",
